-- title:
--   Lemma 3.1 — $\tilde{\mathcal S}_\star$ and $\tilde{\mathcal S}'_\star$ are analytic, and Borel if $\Gamma$ is Borel
-- statement:
--   In the Borel-space MDP with analytic constraint graph $\Gamma$, let $\mathcal S_\star\in\{\mathcal S,\mathcal S_m,\mathcal S_s\}$ and
--   $$\tilde{\mathcal S}_\star=\{(x,p)\in\mathbb X\times\mathcal P(\Omega)\mid p\in\mathcal S_\star,\ p_0(p)=\delta_x\},\qquad\tilde{\mathcal S}'_\star=\{(x,p)\mid p\in\mathcal S'_\star,\ p_0(p)=\delta_x\},$$
--   where $p_0(p)$ is the law of $x_0$ under $p$. Then $\tilde{\mathcal S}_\star$ and $\tilde{\mathcal S}'_\star$ are analytic subsets of $\mathbb X\times\mathcal P(\Omega)$; they are Borel if $\Gamma$ is Borel.
--
--   These sets are the domains of the partial minimization problems in Theorem 3.3, whose sections $\tilde{\mathcal S}_\star(x)$ are the strategic measures feasible from the initial state $x$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: Borel subsets of $\mathbb X\times\mathcal P(\Omega)$ → measurable sets for the product of the Borel $\sigma$-algebra of $\mathbb X$ and Mathlib's $\sigma$-algebra on `ProbabilityMeasure` generated by $p\mapsto p(B)$; reason: that product $\sigma$-algebra is contained in the Borel one (equal for Borel spaces), so the conclusion is at least as strong
--   - encoding: Markov and stationary policies as kernels depending only on $x_n$ → kernels factoring through universally measurable kernels on $\mathbb A$ given $\mathbb X$; reason: the two descriptions give the same policies
--   - encoding: analytic sets as the empty set or images of Borel subsets of Polish spaces under Borel maps (p. 5) → Mathlib's `AnalyticSet` (empty or a continuous image of the Baire space); reason: the two agree for subsets of a Borel space (Bertsekas–Shreve Prop. 7.41)
--   - encoding: $\Gamma$ Borel in $\mathbb X\times\mathbb A$ → `MeasurableSet M.graph` in the product $\sigma$-algebra; reason: Borel spaces are second countable, so the product $\sigma$-algebra is the Borel one
--   - encoding: $\rho_{p_0}[\pi]$ as the restriction of $\mathbb P^\pi_{p_0}$ to $\mathcal B(\Omega)$ → the predicate `IsStrategicMeasure M π p₀ P` ($x_0\sim p_0$, $a_n$ given $h_n$ follows $\mu_n$, $x_{n+1}$ given $h_n'$ follows $q$); reason: the predicate characterizes exactly that measure (compare Lemma 5.1), which exists and is unique by the milestone `strategicMeasure_existsUnique`
--   - generalization: the model includes a lower semianalytic one-stage cost $c$ → the model has no cost; reason: the statement does not involve $c$, so every model of the paper is an instance
-- source:
--   Yu, arXiv:2206.06492v3, Lemma 3.1, p. 8 (S̃⋆, S̃′⋆ defined on p. 7)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BertsekasShreve_AnalyticSelection_BorelSpace
import Definitions.Def_YuStrategic_Optimality_StrategicSets

namespace YuStrategic.Optimality

open MeasureTheory BertsekasShreve.AnalyticSelection

/-- **Lemma 3.1** (Yu, arXiv:2206.06492v3, p. 8). Let `S⋆ ∈ {S, S_m, S_s}` (p. 7). The sets
`S̃⋆ = {(x, p) ∈ X × P(Ω) | p ∈ S⋆, p₀(p) = δₓ}` and `S̃′⋆ = {(x, p) | p ∈ S′⋆, p₀(p) = δₓ}` are
analytic; they are Borel if the graph `Γ` is Borel.

**Formalization Note.** `X × P(Ω)` carries the product of the topology of `X` and the
weak-convergence topology (for "analytic"), and the product of the Borel σ-algebra of `X` and
Mathlib's σ-algebra on `ProbabilityMeasure` generated by `p ↦ p(B)` (for "Borel"). The latter
is contained in the Borel σ-algebra of the product topology (equal for Borel spaces), so the
measurability conclusion is at least as strong as the page's. -/
theorem lemma_3_1 {X A : Type} [TopologicalSpace X] [MeasurableSpace X] [BorelSpace X]
    [IsBorelSpace X] [TopologicalSpace A] [MeasurableSpace A] [BorelSpace A] [IsBorelSpace A]
    (M : Model X A) (κ : PolicyClass) :
    AnalyticSet (stratSetTilde M κ) ∧ AnalyticSet (stratSetTilde' M κ) ∧
      (MeasurableSet M.graph →
        MeasurableSet (stratSetTilde M κ) ∧ MeasurableSet (stratSetTilde' M κ)) := by sorry

end YuStrategic.Optimality
